UPSTREAM MATERIAL KEEPS ITS OWN TERMS

The CC-BY-4.0 grant covers what Geekdojo and contributors wrote: the selection and
arrangement of compounds, system placement, the acts_on / burdens / confounds
distinction, marker selection and cadence, regulatory characterisation, and all prose.
It covers only rights Geekdojo and contributors hold. It does not relicense anything
taken from an upstream source, and it claims nothing the upstream terms do not allow.

Every source row records its own licence in `sources[].license`, and every required
notice is reproduced in docs/ATTRIBUTION.md and in the `attribution` block of every
published bundle:

  openFDA / DailyMed SPL (U.S. FDA)                   CC0-1.0 / U.S. public domain
  UniTox (Silberg, Simon, Swanson & Zou, Stanford)    CC-BY-4.0
  DILIrank 2.0 (U.S. FDA / NCTR)                      U.S. public domain
  LiverTox (NIDDK, NCBI Bookshelf)                    U.S. public domain
  PubMed citations                                    identifiers only; no text reproduced

- Public-domain and CC0 material stays unrestricted. This licence adds no condition to
  it; take it from its source, or from here, on its own terms.
- UniTox ratings are licensed CC-BY-4.0 by their own authors. Anyone redistributing
  them, whether from this repository, from a bundle, or from anything built on either,
  owes the UniTox attribution in docs/ATTRIBUTION.md as well as ours. Keeping a bundle's
  `attribution` block intact satisfies both.
- The openFDA disclaimer in docs/ATTRIBUTION.md travels with label-derived data.


HOW TO ATTRIBUTE THIS CATALOG

  human-system-substance-catalog by Geekdojo and contributors,
  https://github.com/geekdojo/human-system-substance-catalog, licensed under CC BY 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Changes: <say what you changed, if anything>.

and carry the upstream notices from the bundle's `attribution` block, or from
docs/ATTRIBUTION.md, alongside it.


WHAT THIS REPOSITORY IS NOT

This repository contains reference data about compounds. It describes no person,
contains no health data, and is not medical advice, a medical device, or a clinical
decision support system. It describes what to monitor. Nothing in it recommends
starting, stopping, or changing any dose. Interpretation belongs to a physician.

Both licences disclaim all warranties and limit liability; see Section 5 of
LICENSE-DATA and Sections 7 and 8 of LICENSE-CODE.
